Abstract
Based on the TDMU port-recursive axiom system (Rules 1–8) and the MJP-τ(V) critical phase-locking condition, this paper constructs a continuous temporal evolution sequence from surface inorganic light elements to an organized living system with autonomous internal circulation. This sequence presupposes no RNA world, relies on no extraterrestrial organic input, and requires no clay mineral catalysis. Instead, it interprets the emergence of life as a series of recursive geometric phase transitions driven by decreasing energy scales during Earth's cooling process. The core mechanisms include: electrostatic condensation of carbonate ions forming micro-reactors, recursive stress screening within droplets assembling a minimal protocell, ocean current waste removal and tidal oscillations driving metabolic cycles, community-level fragmentation reproduction enabling information transfer, and necrotic cavity-induced primordial internal circulatory pumping. This framework demotes self-replication from an individual property to an emergent group-level phenomenon, transforming the origin of life from a "rare chance event" into a "deterministic sequence of phase transitions upon satisfaction of boundary conditions."

1. Introduction
1.1 Problem Statement
Research on the origin of life has long faced three major dilemmas:

The Water Paradox: Polymerization (condensation) reactions are inhibited in liquid water by competitive hydrolysis.

The Dilution Paradox: Reactant concentrations in the ocean are extremely low, resulting in insufficient collision frequencies.

The Replication Paradox: Complex enzyme systems are required to replicate informational molecules, yet enzymes themselves are products of informational molecules.

This paper attempts to simultaneously resolve these dilemmas within a unified axiomatic framework, reinterpreting the emergence of life as a recursive phase transition sequence driven by the coupling of port geometry and fluid dynamics, triggered as Earth's cooling curve crosses specific energy-scale windows.

